columns in $M$ equals the number of rows in $L,$ the matrices $M$ and $L$ are conformable for multiplication, and $ML$ is a $4\\times 6$ matrix:\n\n$$\n{M}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{L}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 6}={ML}_{4\\times 6}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices Given the Dimensions of the Factors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124639 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124197 :question/id #uuid "35a664b8-a895-5c52-a92f-b62c75b44744" :question/math-academy-id "e-11026" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ given that $f(x,y) = 3x\\ln y + 2ye^x.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =3x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+2{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}+2{e}^{x}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{y}+2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53c5d5d7-40d4-5210-8794-428cffc1f3f9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749 :topic/math-academy-id 1114 :topic/title "Differentiating Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420 :topic/math-academy-id 280 :topic/title "Differentiating Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675 :topic/math-academy-id 303 :topic/title "Differentiating Inverse Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf04fd45-6472-58ca-94d4-d15d2e211452" :answer/type {:db/ident :a