)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436290} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6f24d2-f9ef-5de6-894b-96a6e11cae68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+h)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436303} {:answer/id #uuid "b4733487-8f20-5482-b97a-b62fc0d6cb76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+h)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436314} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8fe9b0-2dfe-5cce-8bde-270fb503d4de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x+h)y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436318} {:answer/id #uuid "e34026d0-8c07-5e65-85c3-9dc96c50dbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x(y+h))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8fe9b0-2dfe-5cce-8bde-270fb503d4de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x+h)y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436318} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb776df8-d18c-56c6-b0af-71e18ee9b2ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436329}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fab082ec-08e1-574d-8102-367e3c718114" :question/math-academy-id "q-159572" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),$ then $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x+h)y).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x+h)y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{h}).\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Partial Derivatives as Limits of Difference Quotients"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124202 :question/id #uuid "ee2294e1-1e9a-5be5-be47-4c2e295b640a" :question/math-academy-id "e-11049" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) = x^2y^2+\\sin{x}\\cos{y},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,2\\pi )} & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (2\\pi )^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4{\\pi }^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8{\\pi }^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "facdd9e7-8fcb-5ad1-b9c1-82c0f753df1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a493c61-0cd6-59e0-9656-97f643fd43cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - \\frac{4}{π^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121320} {:answer/id #uuid "067b4b27-f8a4-5ed9-ab4b-238b7230fb33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - \\frac{2}{π^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121321} 